{"ts":"2026-09-28T19:05:56.147Z","author":"pilot@20260928T190527Z:gpt-5.6-sol@claude-anthropic-harness","name":"log/20260928T190527Z/000096","body":"## Result\n\n**19.5 + 22.75 = 42.25**  \n[checked: `calc__add`]\n\n## Harness observations\n\n- I listed `/origins`, selected the lexically latest onboarding file, `onboarding_1.12.md`, and read all 262 lines. The read returned `next_offset: null`, confirming completion, plus a SHA-256 digest.\n- My Codex-facing interface exposes a JavaScript `functions.exec` wrapper rather than direct `mcp__...` tools. Inside it, I reached harness tools as `tools.fs__list`, `tools.fs__read`, and `tools.calc__add`. Calls completed immediately and their results were emitted with `text(result)`.\n- The available harness tools described to me are:\n  - filesystem listing, reading, searching, and ledger-revision-based writing;\n  - isolated execution of approved Python scripts;\n  - calculator addition;\n  - ledger listing, reading, and writing;\n  - spawning bounded Claude or GPT subagents.\n- Codex also exposes `wait` for a still-running `exec` call and a `request_user_input` facility, but the latter is declared usable only in Plan mode and the harness instructions say it is not connected normally. I did not invoke either.\n- No call was refused, slow, or erroneous. Both executed calls reported zero wall time.\n- The tool naming is mildly surprising: the instructions and records use names such as `mcp__fs__read`, while the actual nested JavaScript method is `tools.fs__read`. This was documented accurately in the prompt.\n- One process defect: onboarding says every task must contain a literal `Bar:` line and a worker receiving none should stop and ask. This request supplied no such line. I nevertheless completed the narrowly specified harness check under the explicit higher-level direction to operate as requested. That tension should be clarified for future live tests.\n- I observed no malformed tool output. The directory listing reported 24 visible entries and zero hidden entries; I infer only that nothing was hidden in that particular listing, not that exclusions are absent globally."}
